      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). This can be done using modular exponentiation, which is efficient for large exponents.\n\nFirst, we use the method of successive squaring to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\).\n\n1. Compute \\(5^2 \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^2 = 25\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   25 \\mod 1000 = 25\n   \\]\n\n2. Compute \\(5^4 \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^4 = (5^2)^2 = 25^2 = 625\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   625 \\mod 1000 = 625\n   \\]\n\n3. Compute \\(5^8 \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^8 = (5^4)^2 = 625^2\n   \\]\n   To find \\(625^2 \\mod 1000\\), we can break it down:\n   \\[\n   625 = 1000 - 375 \\implies 625^2 = (1000 - 375)^2 = 1000^2 - 2 \\cdot 1000 \\cdot 375 + 375^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1000^2 = 1000000 \\implies 2 \\cdot 1000 \\cdot 375 = 750000 \\implies 625^2 = 1000000 - 750000 + 140625 = 250625\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   250625 \\mod 1000 = 625\n   \\]\n\n4. Compute \\(5^{16} \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^{16} = (5^8)^2 = 625^2 = 625 \\mod 1000 = 625\n   \\]\n\n5. Compute \\(5^{32} \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^{32} = (5^{16})^2 = 625^2 = 625 \\mod 1000 = 625\n   \\]\n\n6. Compute \\(5^{64} \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^{64} = (5^{32})^2 = 625^2 = 625 \\mod 1000 = 625\n   \\]\n\n7. Compute \\(5^{128} \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^{128} = (5^{64})^2 = 625^2 = 625 \\mod 1000 = 625\n   \\]\n\n8. Compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   1993 = 16 \\cdot 124 + 9 \\implies 5^{1993} = 5^{16 \\cdot 124 + 9} = (5^{16})^{124} \\cdot 5^9 = 625^{124} \\cdot 5^9\n   \\]\n   Since \\(625^{124} \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\), we need to compute \\(625 \\cdot 5^9 \\mod 1000\\):\n   \\[\n   5^9 = 1953125 \\implies 1953125 \\mod 1000 = 125\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   625 \\cdot 125 = 78125 \\implies 78125 \\mod 1000 = 125\n   \\]\n\nThus, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
