      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to determine \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We can use Euler's theorem, which states that if \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime, then \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\mod n\\), where \\(\\phi\\) is the Euler's totient function. For \\(n = 1000\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\phi(1000) = \\phi(2^3 \\cdot 5^3) = 1000 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} = 400.\n\\]\nSince \\(5\\) and \\(1000\\) are coprime, Euler's theorem tells us that:\n\\[\n5^{400} \\equiv 1 \\mod 1000.\n\\]\nThus, we can reduce the exponent \\(1993\\) modulo \\(400\\):\n\\[\n1993 \\div 400 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 393 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 1993 \\equiv 393 \\mod 400.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n5^{1993} \\equiv 5^{393} \\mod 1000.\n\\]\nNext, we need to compute \\(5^{393} \\mod 1000\\). We can use successive squaring to simplify the calculation:\n\\[\n5^2 = 25,\n\\]\n\\[\n5^4 = 25^2 = 625,\n\\]\n\\[\n5^8 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000,\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{16} = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000,\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{32} = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000,\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{64} = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000.\n\\]\nNow, we can express \\(5^{393}\\) in terms of these powers:\n\\[\n5^{393} = 5^{360+32+8+1} = (5^{360})(5^{32})(5^8)(5) \\equiv (1)(1)(625)(5) \\mod 1000.\n\\]\nCalculating the right-hand side:\n\\[\n(1)(1)(625)(5) = 3125 \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000.\n\\]\nTherefore, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
